WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.048
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:08.305
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.605 --> 00:00:19.733
Unit forty-eight: Biodiversity and Extinction. In this unit, you will describe biodiversity loss in formal and literary register.

4
00:00:20.717 --> 00:00:23.668
biodiversity hotspot.

5
00:00:24.368 --> 00:00:30.977
biodiversity hotspot means a region rich in species that is also seriously threatened.

6
00:00:31.277 --> 00:00:35.784
The Cardamom Mountains lie within a biodiversity hotspot.

7
00:00:36.484 --> 00:00:40.971
Your turn. Say: biodiversity hotspot.

8
00:00:43.671 --> 00:00:45.938
biodiversity hotspot.

9
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.450
keystone species.

10
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:54.505
keystone species means a species on which many others in an ecosystem depend.

11
00:00:54.805 --> 00:00:58.256
Elephants are a keystone species that shape the forest.

12
00:00:58.956 --> 00:01:02.375
Your turn. Say: keystone species.

13
00:01:05.075 --> 00:01:06.552
keystone species.

14
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:08.841
extinction debt.

15
00:01:09.541 --> 00:01:15.709
extinction debt means future extinctions that are inevitable because of past habitat loss.

16
00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:19.506
Forest loss today creates an extinction debt for tomorrow.

17
00:01:20.206 --> 00:01:23.416
Your turn. Say: extinction debt.

18
00:01:26.116 --> 00:01:27.407
extinction debt.

19
00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:30.068
habitat fragmentation.

20
00:01:30.768 --> 00:01:37.295
habitat fragmentation means the breaking of large habitats into small, isolated pieces.

21
00:01:37.595 --> 00:01:42.242
Roads cause habitat fragmentation and trap animal populations.

22
00:01:42.942 --> 00:01:46.511
Your turn. Say: habitat fragmentation.

23
00:01:49.211 --> 00:01:50.967
habitat fragmentation.

24
00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:53.495
wildlife corridor.

25
00:01:54.195 --> 00:02:00.595
wildlife corridor means a strip of habitat connecting separated areas so animals can move.

26
00:02:00.895 --> 00:02:04.682
A wildlife corridor lets tigers travel between reserves.

27
00:02:05.382 --> 00:02:08.789
Your turn. Say: wildlife corridor.

28
00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:13.025
wildlife corridor.

29
00:02:14.017 --> 00:02:15.238
de-extinction.

30
00:02:15.938 --> 00:02:21.235
de-extinction means attempting to bring extinct species back to life using genetics.

31
00:02:21.535 --> 00:02:24.905
De-extinction of the mammoth remains highly controversial.

32
00:02:25.605 --> 00:02:29.128
Your turn. Say: de-extinction.

33
00:02:31.828 --> 00:02:32.887
de-extinction.

34
00:02:33.883 --> 00:02:34.861
seed vault.

35
00:02:35.561 --> 00:02:39.569
seed vault means a secure store protecting seeds for the future.

36
00:02:39.869 --> 00:02:43.088
The Svalbard seed vault holds over a million samples.

37
00:02:43.788 --> 00:02:46.626
Your turn. Say: seed vault.

38
00:02:49.326 --> 00:02:50.211
seed vault.

39
00:02:51.217 --> 00:02:52.821
invasive species.

40
00:02:53.521 --> 00:02:59.910
invasive species means a non-native species that spreads and harms local ecosystems.

41
00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.985
Water hyacinth is an invasive species in many rivers.

42
00:03:04.685 --> 00:03:08.510
Your turn. Say: invasive species.

43
00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:12.862
invasive species.

44
00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:16.093
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:16.393 --> 00:03:20.901
Ms. Sarah, how would a scientist describe what is happening to nature?

46
00:03:22.217 --> 00:03:27.853
Formally: 'Habitat fragmentation constitutes the principal driver of species decline.'

47
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.163
And a poet?

48
00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:36.889
Perhaps: 'The forest falls silent, and the last song fades from the hills.'

49
00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:41.367
Both describe the extinction debt, in different ways.

50
00:03:42.683 --> 00:03:47.240
Indeed. The former informs; the latter moves the heart.

51
00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:49.760
Which works better?

52
00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:56.405
Ere we lose more keystone species, we shall need both science and poetry.

53
00:03:57.717 --> 00:04:02.166
Grammar focus: Formal and literary register (extended).

54
00:04:02.466 --> 00:04:05.127
Register changes with purpose and audience.

55
00:04:05.427 --> 00:04:14.395
Formally: habitat fragmentation constitutes the principal driver of species decline. Poetically: the forest falls silent.

56
00:04:14.695 --> 00:04:19.481
Ere we lose more keystone species, we shall need both science and poetry.

57
00:04:19.781 --> 00:04:30.909
Formal register uses precise Latinate verbs; literary register uses imagery and rhythm. Archaic words like 'ere' belong only in poetic or rhetorical style.

58
00:04:31.917 --> 00:04:35.103
Now read and listen. The Silence of the Forest.

59
00:04:35.403 --> 00:04:41.095
Scientists warn that the planet is experiencing a profound loss of biodiversity.

60
00:04:42.383 --> 00:04:50.327
Habitat fragmentation, pollution, climate change and invasive species constitute the principal drivers of this decline.

61
00:04:51.617 --> 00:04:58.922
Many biodiversity hotspots, including parts of Southeast Asia, are particularly vulnerable.

62
00:05:00.217 --> 00:05:07.859
Because of past destruction, ecosystems may already carry an extinction debt that will be paid in future losses.

63
00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:14.087
The disappearance of a keystone species can unravel entire food webs.

64
00:05:15.383 --> 00:05:20.796
Conservationists therefore create wildlife corridors and protect seeds in secure seed vaults.

65
00:05:22.083 --> 00:05:28.843
Some scientists even propose de-extinction, though critics fear it distracts from protecting living species.

66
00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:37.827
As one writer put it, when the forest falls silent, it is not only animals that are lost, but part of our own humanity.

67
00:05:39.117 --> 00:05:41.699
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:41.999 --> 00:05:45.044
A species on which many others depend is a blank.

69
00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:51.714
A, keystone species. B, invasive species. C, biodiversity hotspot.

70
00:05:55.717 --> 00:05:58.227
The answer is A: keystone species.

71
00:05:59.717 --> 00:06:02.668
Which sentence is in formal academic register?

72
00:06:02.968 --> 00:06:11.475
A, Habitat loss constitutes a major threat.. B, Habitat loss is a big deal.. C, The trees weep for their friends..

73
00:06:15.483 --> 00:06:19.828
The answer is A: Habitat loss constitutes a major threat..

74
00:06:21.317 --> 00:06:22.736
'Ere' means blank.

75
00:06:23.036 --> 00:06:26.457
A, before. B, after. C, because.

76
00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:32.798
The answer is A: before.

77
00:06:34.283 --> 00:06:38.257
A strip of habitat connecting separated areas is a blank.

78
00:06:38.557 --> 00:06:44.509
A, wildlife corridor. B, seed vault. C, extinction debt.

79
00:06:48.517 --> 00:06:51.282
The answer is A: wildlife corridor.

80
00:06:52.783 --> 00:06:57.430
According to the text, what can the disappearance of a keystone species do?

81
00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:04.901
A, unravel entire food webs. B, create new species. C, stop climate change.

82
00:07:08.917 --> 00:07:12.704
The answer is A: unravel entire food webs.

83
00:07:14.217 --> 00:07:20.677
Now it's your turn. Part one: Why should ordinary people care about biodiversity loss?

84
00:07:25.977 --> 00:07:32.679
Part two: Give one sentence in formal register and one in literary register.

85
00:07:38.979 --> 00:07:52.335
Formally: biodiversity provides essential services such as clean water, pollination and food security. Literarily: when the last elephant leaves the Cardamoms, the mountains will lose their heartbeat.

86
00:07:52.635 --> 00:07:55.262
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:02.908
Great work today! You learned eight new words and formal and literary register (extended).

88
00:08:03.208 --> 00:08:08.379
Today's value is caring for nature. Care for plants, birds and our planet.

89
00:08:08.679 --> 00:08:12.121
See you in Unit forty-nine. Goodbye!

90
00:08:12.421 --> 00:08:15.515
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

